S='Recommended Standards for Wastewater Facilities (Ten States Standards), Great Lakes-Upper Mississippi River Board, 2014 edition, Chapter 30 (read 2026-11-20)'
add('std-10ss-sewer-1','US','Ten States Standards for sewers: manhole spacing, drops, alignment, steep slopes (Chapter 30)',
"Ten States Standards (Recommended Standards for Wastewater Facilities, 2014 edition, Chapter 30, Design of Sewers). Many state environmental agencies review sewer plans against this book or their own version of it. Manhole spacing (34.1): no more than 400 feet for sewers 15 inches and smaller, 500 feet for 18 to 30 inches, and up to 600 feet where modern cleaning equipment is available. Drop manhole (34.2): a drop pipe is needed when the incoming sewer is 24 inches or more above the manhole invert; under 24 inches the invert is filleted. Manhole size (34.3): at least 48 inches in diameter with a 24-inch minimum access opening. Alignment (33.5): sewers 24 inches and smaller are laid straight between manholes and checked by laser or lamping. Slope must be uniform between manholes (33.44). Velocities over 10 feet per second need protection against scour (33.45). Sewers on slopes of 20 percent or more are anchored: every 36 feet on 20 to 35 percent grades, 24 feet on 35 to 50 percent, 16 feet at 50 percent and steeper (33.46). Your state's own design rule and the project specs govern.",S,U,ED)
add('std-10ss-sewer-2','US','Ten States Standards sewer tests: 100 gallon leakage limit, air test standards, 5 percent deflection, manhole test',
"Ten States Standards (Recommended Standards for Wastewater Facilities, 2014 edition). Leakage tests (33.9): by water, leakage in or out must not exceed 100 gallons per inch of pipe diameter per mile per day, tested with at least 2 feet of positive head (33.94); by air, follow ASTM C828 for clay pipe, ASTM C924 for concrete pipe and ASTM F1417 for plastic pipe (33.95). Deflection test (33.85): required for all flexible pipe, run after the final backfill has been in place at least 30 days; no pipe may exceed 5 percent deflection; the rigid ball or mandrel is at least 95 percent of the pipe's base or average inside diameter and is pulled by hand, with no mechanical pulling devices. Manholes (34.7): inspected and tested for watertightness before going into service; air (vacuum) testing of concrete manholes follows ASTM C1244. Project specs are often tighter than these numbers, so read the testing section of your contract.",S,U,ED)
add('std-10ss-sep-1','US','Ten States Standards: sewer and water main separation, 10 feet and 18 inches (Section 38)',
"Ten States Standards (Recommended Standards for Wastewater Facilities, 2014 edition, Section 38, protection of water supplies). Side by side (38.31): sewers are laid at least 10 feet horizontally from any existing or proposed water main, measured edge to edge. Where 10 feet is not practical the reviewing agency can allow closer on a case-by-case basis if the water main is in a separate trench, or on an undisturbed earth shelf at one side of the sewer trench, with the bottom of the water main at least 18 inches above the top of the sewer. If neither can be done, both lines are built of slip-on or mechanical joint pipe rated at least 150 psi and pressure tested. Crossings (38.32): at least 18 inches between the outside of the water main and the outside of the sewer, whether the water main is above or below; center a length so the sewer joints are as far as possible from the water main joints; where a water main passes under a sewer, the sewer needs structural support. If 18 inches cannot be met, either build the sewer to water pipe standards and pressure test it, or encase the water main or the sewer in a watertight carrier pipe that extends 10 feet on both sides of the crossing. No water pipe may pass through or touch any part of a sewer manhole (38.1). State rules vary, so check the state health or environmental agency's separation rule for the job.",S,U,ED)
U='https://deq.mt.gov/files/Water/WQInfo/Documents/Circulars/Circulars/2022DEQ-1_FINAL.pdf'
ED='2022'
S='Montana DEQ Circular DEQ-1, Standards for Water Works, 2022 edition, Chapter 8 (read 2026-11-20)'
add('spec-mt-water-1','MT','Montana water main rules: pressure, hydrants, sewer separation (Circular DEQ-1, Chapter 8)',
"Montana (DEQ Circular DEQ-1, Standards for Water Works, 2022 edition, Chapter 8). Pressure (8.2.1): at least 35 psi normal working pressure and at least 20 psi under all flow conditions; normal working pressure should be about 60 to 80 psi. Size (8.2.2): mains serving fire hydrants are at least 6 inches; at least 3 inches where there is no fire protection. Hydrants (8.4): lead at least 6 inches with an auxiliary valve; hydrant drains may not connect to, or be within 10 feet of, sanitary sewers or storm drains. Installation (8.7): continuous uniform bedding; enough cover to prevent freezing; pressure and leakage tests per the AWWA standards; flushing, disinfection and bacteria testing per AWWA C651. Separation from sewers (8.8): at least 10 feet horizontal, edge to edge, from gravity sanitary or storm sewers (8.8.2); at least 18 inches vertical at crossings, outside to outside (8.8.3), with reduced crossings down to 6 inches only under listed conditions such as a 150 psi tested sewer and encasement or flowable fill 10 feet each side; force mains 10 feet horizontal and 18 inches vertical (8.8.4); no water pipe through or touching a sewer manhole, and mains should be at least 10 feet from manholes (8.8.5). Unprotected cross-connections are not allowed (8.10.1).",S,U,ED)
